<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tabinshwehti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1516">1516</a>–1550</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toungoo-dynasty">Toungoo dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tabinshwehti was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-toungoo-empire">First Toungoo Empire</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> from 1530 to 1550. Starting from the small kingdom of Toungoo, he conquered the Mon kingdom of Hanthawaddy, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> in 1539 and Martaban in 1541, and Prome in 1542, with the help of Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and firearms. He made Pegu his capital but failed to take Arakan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, and was assassinated in 1550; his brother-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> restored the empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tabinshwehti.jpg" alt="Tabinshwehti" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: R. C. Temple · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tabinshwehti_Nat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tabinshwehti was born at Toungoo Palace on 16 April 1516 to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mingyi-nyo">Mingyi Nyo</a> and his concubine Khin Oo, a village chief's daughter. His name means Unitary Golden Umbrella, the golden umbrella being the symbol of Burmese kings. He was trained in martial arts, horse and elephant riding and strategy alongside Ye Htut, the son of his wet nurse, who became his right-hand man. He came to the throne aged 14 when his father died on 24 November 1530.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>About April 1534 Ye Htut's affair with the king's half-sister Thakin Gyi, treason under Burmese law, was discovered; Tabinshwehti gave him the princess in marriage and a princely title, winning lasting loyalty. Late that year he attacked the Hanthawaddy kingdom, probably urged on by older ministers. After raids failed, misinformation led King Takayutpi to execute his own loyal ministers, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> fell without a shot. After a victory at Naungyo, Ye Htut was given the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a>, King's Elder Brother.</p><p>Martaban, defended by Portuguese mercenaries, fell in May 1541 after a seven-month siege; Tabinshwehti had the viceroy, his family and the defenders executed for refusing amnesty. Prome surrendered on 19 May 1542 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> destroyed an Arakanese relief army, and Toungoo forces reached Pagan. Tabinshwehti was crowned there in the manner of the Pagan kings and again at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> in 1545 in the Hanthawaddy tradition, wearing his hair in the Mon style and naming a Mon, Khay Ma Naw, chief queen.</p><p>His invasion of Arakan ended when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/min-bin">Min Bin</a> flooded the besiegers out of Mrauk-U by opening the city's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a>, and a truce followed on 30 January 1547. On 14 October 1548 he led 12,000 men into Siam through the Three Pagodas Pass, but Siamese counterattacks broke the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> in January 1549.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Introduced to wine by a young Portuguese mercenary, he became an alcoholic and handed day-to-day government to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a>. On 30 April 1550, his 34th birthday, swordsmen sent by Smim Sawhtut, governor of Sittaung, beheaded him in his tent during a hunting trip. Each major governor then declared himself independent. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-lieberman">Victor Lieberman</a> called his death &quot;one of the great turning points of mainland [<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>] history&quot;, and the Tabinshwehti nat is one of the 37 spirits worshipped in Myanmar.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tabinshwehti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1548 <b>Tabinshwehti</b> of the Toungoo dynasty besieged the capital, a campaign remembered for the death of Queen Suriyothai in combat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Having restored <b>Tabinshwehti</b>'s empire, Bayinnaung was formally crowned in Pegu with the reign name Thiri Thudhamma Yaza.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-12">12 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Tabinshwehti</b> made Prome a provincial capital again.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-19">19 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A Toungoo force of 12,000 led by <b>Tabinshwehti</b> and Bayinnaung invaded Siam through the Three Pagodas Pass and advanced to Ayutthaya.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-14">14 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>Tabinshwehti</b>, from 1530, conquered the Hanthawaddy Kingdom, made Pegu his capital in 1539 and seized Prome and much of central Myanmar.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-toungoo-empire">First Toungoo Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Internal decline left Hanthawaddy open to the rising First Toungoo Empire, whose king <b>Tabinshwehti</b> took Pegu in 1539 and Martaban in 1541, making Pegu his own capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/hanthawaddy-kingdom">Hanthawaddy Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Taungoo, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in Pantanaw, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.93,96.43&amp;year=1538" aria-label="Taungoo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7159 1462 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tabinshwehti"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7609 1732h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7609 1732h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.93,96.43&amp;year=1538">What was here in 1538? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
